<!--
    kuna castimplied (P9, sub-stage cast-policy; option castimplied on|off).

    Source: the 2026-09-23 cast census.  CastStrategyC::isExtensionCastImplied
    (cast.cc:249) hides an extension only under integer arithmetic, so every
    widening that reaches a call argument, an assignment or a return prints as
    a cast even where C performs exactly that conversion itself.

    gcc -O0 -fno-pie of these static functions (the bytes below are .text):

      sinki(int x)             declared below: extern int4 sinki(int4 x)
      sinkl(long x)            no declaration: its prototype is a trial
      prf(const char *f, ...)  declared below with a varargs tail
      argi(char c)       { return sinki(c); }
      argl(int i)        { return sinkl(i); }
      vararg(f, int i)   { return prf(f, (long)i); }
      asg(s, n)          { ... long x; x = s[i]; sum += x * x + x; ... }
      ret(int i)         { return i; }
      stacked(t, char c) { int i = to_uchar(c); return t[i]; }
      keepinner(u, o)    { *o = u >> 1; return (long)(int)u; }

    Pass 1 (option castimplied off) is the upstream rendering: #1, #5, #7, #9
    and #11 show a cast C would perform by itself.  Pass 2 (option castimplied
    on) leaves those out (#2, #6, #8, #10, #12): the declared int4 parameter
    converts the char argument, the int8 local converts the loaded char, the
    int8 return converts the int4, and the (uint4) widening under (int4) keeps
    the value the (uint1) cast produced.  Both passes keep the casts that change
    the value or that C does not perform: an argument to a callee with no
    declared prototype (#3), a varargs argument (#4), the (uint1) sign change
    under the widening (#10) and the (int4) sign change under a dropped
    widening (#12).
-->
